---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import WaistToHipRatioCalculator from '@/components/tools/calc/WaistToHipRatioCalculator.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="waist-to-hip-ratio-calculator">
  <WaistToHipRatioCalculator client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How the waist-to-hip ratio calculator works</h2>
    <p>
      The waist-to-hip ratio calculator divides your waist by your hip: <code>WHR = waist ÷ hip</code>. Both
      measurements must be in the same unit, and the ratio has no unit. A high ratio means more fat around
      the abdomen, which is linked to heart disease and type 2 diabetes.
    </p>
    <p>
      The World Health Organization measures the waist midway between the lowest rib and the top of the hip
      bone, and the hip around the widest part of the buttocks. Its 2008 expert report sets a substantially
      increased risk of metabolic complications at a ratio of 0.90 or more for men and 0.85 or more for
      women.
    </p>
    <p>
      The gauge uses a common three-band scale. Men: 0.95 or less is low risk, 0.96 to 1.00 moderate and
      above 1.00 high. Women: 0.80 or less is low risk, 0.81 to 0.85 moderate and above 0.85 high. If you
      enter your height, the calculator also gives the waist-to-height ratio,
      <code>WHtR = waist ÷ height</code>. A value under 0.5 is the usual low-risk cut-off.
    </p>
    <h2>Worked example</h2>
    <p>
      A man with an 86 cm waist and 100 cm hips has a waist-to-hip ratio of 86 ÷ 100 = 0.86. That is in the
      low-risk band and below the WHO cut-off of 0.90. At 178 cm tall, his waist-to-height ratio is
      86 ÷ 178 = 0.48, under 0.5. A woman with the same hips and an 80 cm waist has a ratio of 0.80, also
      low risk and below her cut-off of 0.85.
    </p>
    <h2>Limits</h2>
    <p>
      The waist-to-hip ratio is a screening measure and does not diagnose a disease. Most of the cut-offs come
      from studies of white European adults; for some Asian groups the risk starts at a lower ratio. The
      ratio is not valid in pregnancy or for children. A small change in tape position changes the result,
      so measure the same way each time.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
